from common import S,X
FORCE="A force is a push or a pull::push,pull|It is measured in newtons (N)::newton,newtons,n"
BAL="Balanced forces are equal in size and opposite in direction, so they cancel and motion does not change::equal,opposite,cancel,balanced,no change|Unbalanced forces are not equal, so there is a resultant force and motion changes::unequal,not equal,resultant,unbalanced,changes"
NEWT="An object at rest stays at rest::rest|An object in motion continues in a straight line at constant speed::motion,straight line,constant speed,same speed|Unless acted on by an unbalanced force::unbalanced,force"
ROLL="Friction between the ball and the ground is an unbalanced force acting against the motion::friction,unbalanced,against|So the ball slows down and stops::slows,stops"
SEAT="In a sudden stop the passenger tends to keep moving forward because of inertia::inertia,keep moving,forward,sudden|The seat belt applies a force that slows the body together with the vehicle::belt,force,slows,holds,held|So the passenger does not hit the dashboard or windscreen::dashboard,windscreen,thrown,hit"
SIX="Lever::lever|Pulley::pulley|Inclined plane::inclined plane,ramp,slope|Wedge::wedge|Screw::screw|Wheel and axle::wheel and axle,wheel"
LEVER="Fulcrum::fulcrum|Effort::effort|Load::load"
EFF="Efficiency = (work output ÷ work input) × 100%::work output,work input,100,percent,output,input|Some work is wasted overcoming friction and lifting moving parts::friction,wasted,heat,moving parts"
IMPR="Oil or grease the moving parts such as the chain::oil,grease,lubricat,chain|Keep it clean and free of rust::clean,rust,dust|Service and repair regularly::service,repair,tighten,maintain"
MAGT="Natural magnets such as lodestone::natural,lodestone,magnetite|Artificial magnets (permanent or temporary)::artificial,permanent,temporary|Electromagnets::electromagnet,electromagnets,coil"
QA=[
S("Define force and state the unit in which it is measured",2,"A force is a push or a pull, measured in newtons (N).",FORCE),
S("Define a force.",1,"A force is a push or a pull.","A push or a pull::push,pull"),
S("State the difference between balanced forces and unbalanced forces",2,"Balanced forces are equal in size and opposite in direction so the resultant is zero and motion does not change. Unbalanced forces are unequal so there is a resultant force and the motion changes.",BAL),
S("State one difference between balanced forces and unbalanced forces",1,"Balanced forces cancel so motion does not change; unbalanced forces change the motion of an object.",BAL),
S("Explain why a loaded wheelbarrow is harder to start",2,"A loaded wheelbarrow has more mass and so more inertia, so it is harder to start moving and harder to stop than an empty one.",
  "It has more mass::mass,heavier,heavy|So it has more inertia, resisting any change in motion::inertia,resist,resists"),
S("Describe one activity that shows that unbalanced forces cause motion",2,"Push a toy car on a smooth table: it moves when the push is greater than friction, and a harder push makes it change its motion more quickly.",
  "Push a toy car or object::push,toy car,car,ball|It moves when the push is bigger than friction (unbalanced force); a larger force gives greater change::friction,greater,larger,harder,unbalanced"),
S("State Newton's First Law of motion",3,"An object at rest remains at rest, and an object in motion continues to move in a straight line at constant speed, unless it is acted on by an unbalanced force.",NEWT),
S("Explain why a ball rolling on the ground eventually stops",2,"Friction between the ball and the ground is an unbalanced force that acts against the motion, so it slows and stops.",ROLL),
S("Describe what would happen to a moving ball if there were no friction",2,"It would never stop; it would keep moving in a straight line at constant speed.","It would never stop::never stop,not stop,forever,continue|It would move in a straight line at constant speed::straight line,constant,same speed"),
S("Why is Newton's First Law also called the law of inertia",2,"Inertia is the tendency of an object to resist any change in its state of rest or motion, which is exactly what the first law describes.",
  "Inertia is the tendency of an object to resist change in rest or motion::inertia,resist,tendency,change|The first law describes this behaviour::rest,motion,law"),
S("What is inertia?",2,"Inertia is the tendency of an object to resist any change in its state of rest or of motion.","The tendency of an object to resist change::tendency,resist,resists,resistance|In its state of rest or motion::rest,motion,moving,still"),
S("Explain how a seat belt protects a passenger in terms of inertia",3,"In a sudden stop the passenger tends to keep moving forward because of inertia. The seat belt applies a force that slows the body with the vehicle, so the passenger does not hit the dashboard or windscreen.",SEAT),
S("Why do passengers need to wear seat belts",2,"When a vehicle stops suddenly the passengers keep moving forward because of inertia; the seat belt holds them back so they are not thrown against the dashboard or through the windscreen.",SEAT),
S("Explain why dust leaves a cloth when it is beaten",2,"The cloth moves but the dust tends to stay at rest because of inertia, so it is left behind.","The cloth moves::cloth,moves|The dust tends to stay at rest (inertia) so it is left behind::rest,inertia,left behind,dust"),
S("Describe one application of Newton's First Law at school or at home",2,"Beating a dusty mat, shaking sauce down a bottle and stopping, or phones sliding forward when a vehicle brakes. The object tends to keep its state of motion or rest.",
  "A valid example such as beating a mat, sauce bottle, hammer head or sliding phone::dust,cloth,mat,bottle,sauce,hammer,phone,runner|The object keeps its state of rest or motion (inertia)::inertia,rest,keeps moving,continues,stay"),
S("State two reasons why the law is important to society",2,"It guides vehicle design (seat belts, headrests, air bags); it explains why heavy vehicles need longer to stop, so drivers keep a safe distance; loads must be tied down; it is used in space travel.",
  "It guides vehicle design and safety such as seat belts::vehicle,seat belt,belts,headrest,air bag,safety|It explains stopping distance, tying down loads, sport or space travel::heavy,stop,tied,load,loads,sport,space"),
S("A driver is carrying a heavy load of sand on an open truck",3,"When the truck brakes or turns, the sand tends to keep moving because of inertia and could fly off. Covering and tying down the load keeps it with the truck and protects other road users.",
  "Loads keep moving because of inertia when the truck stops or turns::inertia,keep moving,keeps moving,braking,brakes,turns|Loose sand could fly off and harm others::fly,fall,thrown,harm,injure|Tying down and covering holds it in place::tied,tie,cover,covered,hold,secure"),
S("A learner pushes a trolley and says it needs a continuous push",3,"By the first law a moving object keeps moving at constant speed unless an unbalanced force acts. The trolley slows because friction acts against it, so the push is only needed to overcome friction.",
  "A moving object keeps moving at constant speed unless an unbalanced force acts::keeps moving,continues,constant speed,unbalanced|The trolley slows because of friction::friction,slows|The push is needed only to overcome friction::overcome,balance,friction,push"),
S("Name four magnetic materials and four nonmagnetic materials",8,"Magnetic: iron, steel, nickel, cobalt. Nonmagnetic: wood, plastic, glass, copper, aluminium, paper, cloth.",
  "Iron::iron|Steel::steel|Nickel::nickel|Cobalt::cobalt|Wood::wood|Plastic::plastic|Glass::glass|Copper, aluminium, paper or cloth::copper,aluminium,paper,cloth"),
S("State the rule about poles of magnets that attract and repel",2,"Like poles repel and unlike poles attract.","Like poles repel::like,repel,same|Unlike poles attract::unlike,attract,opposite"),
S("State the law of magnetic poles",2,"Like poles repel and unlike poles attract.","Like poles repel::like,repel,same|Unlike poles attract::unlike,attract,opposite"),
S("Explain why repulsion is a better test for a magnet than attraction",2,"An unmagnetised piece of iron is also attracted by a magnet, but only a magnet can repel another magnet.","A piece of unmagnetised iron is also attracted::unmagnetised,iron,attracted,also|Only a magnet repels another magnet::repel,repels,repulsion"),
S("Describe two uses of magnets in everyday life",2,"A compass needle points north to find direction; magnetic strips hold fridge doors closed; electromagnets lift scrap iron; loudspeakers; MRI scanners.",
  "A use such as compass, fridge door, loudspeaker, crane, cards or MRI::compass,fridge,loudspeaker,crane,scrap,card,mri,bell,motor|A second use::compass,fridge,loudspeaker,crane,scrap,card,mri,bell,motor"),
S("Name two types of magnets",2,"Natural magnets (lodestone) and artificial magnets, and electromagnets.","One type of magnet::natural,artificial,electromagnet,permanent,temporary,lodestone|A second type::natural,artificial,electromagnet,permanent,temporary,lodestone"),
S("Describe how you would test whether an iron bar is a magnet",2,"Bring one end of the bar near a known magnet's pole. If it repels, it is a magnet; attraction alone is not a sure test because unmagnetised iron is also attracted.",
  "Bring it near the pole of a known magnet::known magnet,pole,near,bring|If it repels, it is a magnet (attraction is not a sure test)::repel,repels,repulsion"),
S("Define a simple machine.",2,"A simple machine is a basic device with few or no moving parts that makes work easier by changing the size or direction of a force.","A basic device with few or no moving parts::basic,few,moving parts,simple|It makes work easier by changing a force::easier,force,direction"),
S("List the six simple machines",6,"Lever, pulley, inclined plane, wedge, screw, wheel and axle.",SIX),
S("Name the six simple machines",6,"Lever, pulley, inclined plane, wedge, screw, wheel and axle.",SIX),
S("Name the simple machine that is used in an axe and in a ramp",2,"An axe is a wedge and a ramp is an inclined plane.","Axe: wedge::wedge|Ramp: inclined plane::inclined plane,incline,inclined"),
S("Explain why a wheelbarrow is a compound machine",2,"A compound machine is made of two or more simple machines working together; a wheelbarrow uses a lever and a wheel and axle.","A compound machine has two or more simple machines::two,more,simple machines,combination|A wheelbarrow combines a lever and a wheel and axle::lever,wheel,axle"),
S("Name the three parts of a lever",3,"The fulcrum, the effort and the load.",LEVER),
S("Give one example of a first, second and third class lever",3,"First class: seesaw. Second class: wheelbarrow. Third class: broom.","First class: seesaw, crowbar, scissors or pliers::seesaw,crowbar,scissors,pliers,hammer|Second class: wheelbarrow, nutcracker or bottle opener::wheelbarrow,nutcracker,opener|Third class: broom, fishing rod or tweezers::broom,fishing rod,tweezers,forearm"),
S("Explain why a wheelbarrow makes the lifting of a load easier",2,"A wheelbarrow is a second class lever with the load between the fulcrum (wheel) and the effort, so the effort arm is longer than the load arm and a small effort lifts a large load.",
  "It is a second class lever with the load between the wheel (fulcrum) and the effort::second class,load,fulcrum,between,wheel|The effort arm is longer than the load arm so a small effort lifts a large load::longer,effort arm,small effort,large load"),
S("A load of 400 N is 0.5 m from the fulcrum",2,"Effort × 2 = 400 × 0.5 = 200, so effort = 200 ÷ 2 = 100 N.","Effort × effort arm = load × load arm::effort,load,arm,x,×|Effort = 100 N::100"),
S("A load of 300 N is lifted using a crowbar",2,"Effort × 1.5 = 300 × 0.4 = 120, so effort = 120 ÷ 1.5 = 80 N.","Effort × effort arm = load × load arm::effort,load,arm,x,×|Effort = 80 N::80"),
S("Write the formula for efficiency",3,"Efficiency = (work output ÷ work input) × 100%, where work output is the useful work done by the machine and work input is the work done on the machine.",
  "Efficiency = (work output ÷ work input) × 100%::output,input,100,percent|Work output is the useful work done by the machine::useful,output,done by|Work input is the work done on the machine by the effort::input,effort,done on"),
S("A machine has a work input of 400 J",2,"Efficiency = 300 ÷ 400 × 100 = 75%.","300 divided by 400 times 100::300,400,100|75 percent::75"),
S("A machine with an efficiency of 70 percent",2,"Work output = 70 ÷ 100 × 1000 = 700 J.","70 percent of 1000 J::70,1000|Output is 700 J::700"),
S("Explain why the efficiency of a machine is always less than 100 percent",2,"Some of the work put in is wasted overcoming friction between moving parts and lifting the parts of the machine, and it appears as heat and sound, so the work output is always less than the work input.",
  "Some work is wasted overcoming friction and lifting moving parts::friction,wasted,waste,moving parts|It appears as heat and sound so output is less than input::heat,sound,less,output,input"),
S("Why is the efficiency of a machine always less than 100 percent?",2,"Some work is wasted overcoming friction and appears as heat and sound.","Some work is wasted overcoming friction::friction,wasted,waste|As heat and sound::heat,sound"),
S("Describe two ways of improving the efficiency of a bicycle",2,"Oil or grease the chain and moving parts to reduce friction; keep it clean and free of rust; service it regularly.",IMPR),
]
